{
  "text": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its Borel measurable structure, every probability measure μ on E, and every real parameter η > 0, define R : E × E → E × E by R(x,y) = (x, 2x − y). Let X have law μ and let Z independently have the centered standard Gaussian law on E with identity covariance. Then R is an involution on all of E × E, meaning R(R(x,y)) = (x,y) for every x,y ∈ E, and R preserves the joint law ν of (X, X + √η Z): R₊ν = ν, where R₊ denotes pushforward. Equivalently, (X, X − √η Z) and (X, X + √η Z) have the same joint distribution. No restriction on μ beyond being a probability measure is assumed; in particular no density or moment assumption is present. The coefficient 2 is fixed, and the Gaussian displacement scale is the nonnegative square root √η; there are no additional constants. The asserted scope is η > 0. All pushforward maps involved are measurable, so Measure.map uses ordinary pushforward and its totalized nonmeasurable fallback is not invoked.",
  "decoder": "gaussian_blind_decoder",
